begin;

with revised as (
  select
    $article$My route to Internal Medicine was not a straight line. Before medicine, I studied the humanities and behavioral sciences, worked in social services as a case manager, helped with clinical research, and completed graduate work in education. Those experiences taught me to see a patient as more than a collection of organs, and that is exactly what eventually drew me to Internal Medicine. The field asks you to understand every organ system and, more importantly, the way those systems affect one another. The familiar image of a doctor in a long white coat piecing together a complicated hospital case is often an internist: someone who can manage the whole picture, invite a cardiologist or gastroenterologist into one part of it, coordinate with nurses and pharmacists, and still remain responsible for where the patient is going next. I did not enter medical school with one dramatic moment of certainty. I liked Pediatrics because I enjoyed rounding and overseeing a team; I liked the focus on women’s health in Obstetrics and Gynecology; I loved the hands-on clarity of Surgery; and I was fascinated by Neurology until a stroke rotation showed me that enjoying neuroanatomy was not the same as wanting the daily practice. The pattern continued until I realized that what I loved was the complete story. I wanted to talk to patients, think deeply about physiology, call experts without needing to be the expert in everything, and follow what happened after a procedure rather than fix one problem and say goodbye. A multidisciplinary round finally made the choice feel real. The attending, resident, student, nurse, social worker, and case manager were not discussing only today’s diagnosis. They were asking whether the person could return home, needed rehabilitation, could obtain medication, had enough support, or required addiction treatment. I recognized that work immediately. Internal Medicine felt like medicine, social care, systems thinking, and human connection gathered around the same patient. It also offered a three-year route to greater independence, with the option to practice general Internal Medicine or continue into fields such as cardiology, gastroenterology, nephrology, and many others. I sometimes miss the operating room, but I do not miss it enough to give up the intellectual breadth, relationships, and responsibility that make this work feel like mine.$article$ as paragraph_1,
    $article$An inpatient day begins before the hospital fully wakes. As an intern, I usually arrived between 6:00 and 6:30 a.m. to receive sign-out from the night team: who needed an extra medication dose, who became confused or hypotensive, whose pain worsened, what test returned, and what the covering resident did about it. Then came the chart review, new laboratory values and imaging, bedside examinations, problem lists, and the first pass at notes, all before rounds at about 8:30. An intern who prefers a slower pace can arrive earlier; someone with a reliable system may come a little later, but the work must be ready when the team gathers. As a senior resident, my day often starts around 7:00. I do not need to repeat every intern task. I scan every chart, make sure I understand the entire service, see the sickest patients and anyone having a particularly difficult morning, answer the interns’ questions, and prepare to lead. Rounds are the heart—and sometimes the comedy—of Internal Medicine. A traditional team may stand outside each room for hours, presenting every detail of what is happening today, what could happen tomorrow, and what the patient will need long after discharge. During the pandemic, many teams shifted toward table rounds: everyone reviewed the chart together, residents entered orders and requested medications or tests immediately, and the attending later visited the sickest patients with the interns and students. Attendings may prefer bedside rounds because they can see everyone once, write their notes, and move into teaching; residents often prefer table rounds because decisions become actions before the to-do list grows. I would choose a hybrid—enough bedside contact to keep the patient at the center, enough efficiency to prevent rounds from swallowing conference, documentation, and the rest of the day. After rounds, the neat presentation becomes messy real life: reassess patients, chase results, update families, write notes, arrange follow-up, and call consultants. I used to be nervous making those calls. By senior year, many fellows were former co-residents or friends, and I had learned how to ask clearly: this is the clinical question, this is what we have tried, this is how urgent it is, and this is where we need help. Sometimes it is a formal consult; sometimes colleagues curbside one another about insulin, fluids, or another focused problem. If someone responds sharply, I bring the conversation back to its purpose: we are colleagues asking for help for a patient. There is no pride in pretending to know what I do not know.$article$ as paragraph_2,
    $article$The rhythm changes completely from one rotation to another. Internal Medicine programs may use a traditional schedule, where residents remain largely on hospital services and leave for continuity clinic one half-day each week, or an “X+Y” model that separates inpatient and ambulatory blocks. My program uses four inpatient weeks followed by two outpatient weeks. During the four, you put your head down and live inside a general medicine or specialty service, perhaps concentrating on cardiac physiology and treatments on a cardiology rotation. During the two, the pace relaxes into what I call residency’s diastole: an opportunity to refill, think about prevention and primary care, and usually work something closer to 8:00 or 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. Continuity clinic is not a break from real medicine. It is a panel of patients you follow from intern year through graduation. Over time, you know their blood-pressure plan before opening the chart, remember their family, understand what they will or will not accept, and become their primary doctor. Clinics generally close on weekends and holidays, which makes ambulatory time feel more like the life of people outside residency. Inpatient weekends are different and vary enough that every applicant should ask detailed questions. In my four-week block, one weekend is fully off—a “golden weekend”—one is fully worked, and two are split so Saturday or Sunday remains free. That produces four weekend days worked and four off, announced early enough to plan a life. Night float is another world. On a 9:00 p.m. to 9:00 a.m. shift, I admitted people arriving through the emergency department, cared for them through the night, and responded to emergencies anywhere in the hospital. During my second year, one call became a workplace-violence crisis involving hospital employees. We responded, then sheltered in place while incomplete information and rumors moved through the building. Returning the next day was difficult, but patients still needed care. The experience made burnout, safety, and the effect of violence on access to care painfully concrete; it also reminded me that many patients live much closer to violence than I usually do. Residency asks for stamina, but its schedules still sit within formal limits: current ACGME requirements cap clinical and educational work at 80 hours per week averaged across four weeks and require a longitudinal, team-based continuity experience. Later, hospital medicine can offer another distinctive rhythm—often a week on and a week off. The on-week includes its weekends and can be intense; the off-week can restore energy, make room for writing, research, teaching, or family, and sometimes allow moonlighting, whose hours still count toward work limits. That alternation appeals to me because few physicians are only one thing.$article$ as paragraph_3,
    $article$The biggest surprise was how much of my day would happen beyond the bedside. I expected complex families and difficult discharge decisions; I did not expect quite so many prior authorizations, peer-to-peer calls with insurers, medication-coverage problems, and negotiations over what care a patient qualifies to receive. My background in case management, advocacy, policy, humanities, and education helps me make sense of it. Population health, public policy, insurance design, and medical economics are not optional topics floating outside clinical medicine. They determine whether the plan we write can become the care a patient receives. That is why I want a career combining academic hospital medicine, teaching, and curricula that place health-system science and humanism beside physiology. The people remain the best part: patients who trust you with frightening moments, students who are still discovering what kind of doctor they might become, residents who grow into confident leaders, and consultants who teach you where their expertise begins. Second year was my favorite stage because I knew the system, could work efficiently, still shared services with close friends, and had not yet scattered into separate fellowship and career tracks. By third year, greater independence felt good, but camaraderie required more deliberate effort. Training also changed me outside the hospital. My first year coincided with the pandemic: our class could not gather, patient loss was heavy, and isolation made it easy to abandon running, team sports, and other parts of myself in favor of sleep or more reading. If I could begin again, I would schedule those sources of wellbeing as necessities, not bonuses. I would also learn the word “no” earlier. Interesting projects, committee work, writing, research, and seemingly harmless organizing tasks all consume time; some advance your purpose, some belong with a colleague who has the interest and bandwidth, and some simply need a polite refusal. This is especially important when service work is repeatedly placed on young physicians without recognition or career value. For students choosing a specialty, my advice is to experience the real work rather than rely on television, stereotypes, or rumors. Watch the operation, visit the endoscopy suite, join the multidisciplinary round, and notice where you enjoy the people and the ordinary Tuesday—not only the exciting case. If you choose Internal Medicine, do not hide the unusual parts of your story to appear more scientific. Humanities, advocacy, prior careers, media, family life, and hobbies can explain how you will connect with patients and contribute to a program. The doctors I admire most are not those who perform certainty. They say, “I know this part, I am not sure about that part, and we can look it up together.” That honesty creates trust, protects patients, and turns medicine back into what it should be: lifelong learning in service of another human being.$article$ as paragraph_4
), updated as (
  update public.blog_posts blog
  set
    title = 'Inside the Ward: Dr Maya’s Life as an Internal Medicine Resident',
    excerpt = 'Dr Maya takes readers from predawn sign-out through rounds, consults, discharge barriers, continuity clinic, night float, career decisions, and the work of remaining human during Internal Medicine residency.',
    content = concat_ws(
      E'\n\n',
      revised.paragraph_1,
      revised.paragraph_2,
      revised.paragraph_3,
      revised.paragraph_4
    ),
    paragraph_1 = revised.paragraph_1,
    paragraph_2 = revised.paragraph_2,
    paragraph_3 = revised.paragraph_3,
    paragraph_4 = revised.paragraph_4,
    author_name = 'Dr Maya',
    updated_at = now()
  from revised
  where blog.slug = 'day-in-the-life-internal-medicine-resident'
  returning blog.id
)
select count(*) from updated;

do $$
declare
  article_count integer;
  article_words integer;
begin
  select
    count(*),
    max(array_length(regexp_split_to_array(btrim(content), E'\\s+'), 1))
  into article_count, article_words
  from public.blog_posts
  where slug = 'day-in-the-life-internal-medicine-resident'
    and title = 'Inside the Ward: Dr Maya’s Life as an Internal Medicine Resident'
    and author_name = 'Dr Maya'
    and published
    and paragraph_1 not ilike '%fictional%'
    and paragraph_1 not ilike '%generalized%'
    and content not ilike '%Bennett%'
    and content not ilike '%Jefferson%'
    and content not ilike '%Philadelphia%'
    and content not ilike '%Penn State%';

  if article_count <> 1 or article_words < 1700 then
    raise exception
      'Expected one expanded Dr Maya article of at least 1,700 words; found % article(s) and % words',
      article_count,
      coalesce(article_words, 0);
  end if;
end;
$$;

commit;
